WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03.652 --> 00:00:07.451
Today is September 25, 2026.

3
00:00:07.451 --> 00:00:14.510
Overnight, the ten-year Treasury yield broke through five and one percent, the highest since 2007.

4
00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:19.400
The five-year crossed five percent for the first time since 2006.

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:24.596
The thirty-year spiked to five and forty-four percent, a 2004 record.

6
00:00:24.596 --> 00:00:31.986
In Tokyo, the ten-year Japanese government bond hit three and eight percent, the highest since 1996.

7
00:00:31.986 --> 00:00:36.704
Osaka Exchange actually halted long-dated bond futures trading.

8
00:00:36.704 --> 00:00:39.290
The signal is unambiguous.

9
00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:43.125
The market is no longer pricing a Fed policy path.

10
00:00:43.125 --> 00:00:46.692
It is pricing a fiscal sustainability crisis.

11
00:00:46.692 --> 00:00:48.971
The divergence is stark.

12
00:00:48.971 --> 00:00:53.224
Wall Street consensus still assumes a gradual normalization.

13
00:00:53.224 --> 00:00:59.437
But the bond market is actively underwriting a scenario where the ten-year stabilizes at six percent.

14
00:00:59.437 --> 00:01:04.057
At that level, the risk-reward versus equities shifts dramatically.

15
00:01:04.057 --> 00:01:07.831
Long-duration growth stocks become the most vulnerable.

16
00:01:07.831 --> 00:01:12.476
JPMorgan has flagged a shift in the AI valuation break-point.

17
00:01:12.476 --> 00:01:16.998
It has moved from five percent to the five-and-a-half to six percent zone.

18
00:01:16.998 --> 00:01:20.417
You are now operating inside that danger zone.

19
00:01:20.417 --> 00:01:23.518
Three forces are driving this reprice.

20
00:01:23.518 --> 00:01:26.777
First, US-Iran talks have stalled.

21
00:01:26.777 --> 00:01:31.937
Tehran has warned that any renewed strikes will extend conflict into the Indian Ocean.

22
00:01:31.937 --> 00:01:34.706
Oil is pushing inflation back up.

23
00:01:34.706 --> 00:01:38.812
Second, Japan is in a borrow-to-repay death spiral.

24
00:01:38.812 --> 00:01:41.508
The yen sits near one-sixty.

25
00:01:41.508 --> 00:01:45.025
Treasury Secretary Bessent's intervention failed.

26
00:01:45.025 --> 00:01:49.351
Japan, a core foreign holder of Treasuries, is selling.

27
00:01:49.351 --> 00:01:53.420
Multiple sovereigns are doing the same to defend their currencies.

28
00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.030
Foreign demand is evaporating.

29
00:01:56.030 --> 00:02:01.005
Third, global debt has crossed three hundred sixty-five trillion dollars.

30
00:02:01.005 --> 00:02:04.523
The US deficit cycle is self-reinforcing.

31
00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:09.694
More issuance, higher yields, more interest cost, more deficit.

32
00:02:09.694 --> 00:02:11.716
The loop is tightening.

33
00:02:11.716 --> 00:02:13.910
The Fed is not helping.

34
00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:18.493
New York Fed President Williams called the economy stronger than expected.

35
00:02:18.493 --> 00:02:22.010
He said another hike this year is a reasonable option.

36
00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:25.760
Powell acknowledged inflation progress is insufficient.

37
00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.885
The market is now pricing an October hike.

38
00:02:28.885 --> 00:02:31.128
The cut cycle is dead.

39
00:02:31.128 --> 00:02:34.008
US PMI data keeps beating.

40
00:02:34.008 --> 00:02:39.523
The GDP nowcast for Q3 sits at five point one percent annualized.

41
00:02:39.523 --> 00:02:41.937
The economy is overheating.

42
00:02:41.937 --> 00:02:45.086
Higher for longer is no longer a slogan.

43
00:02:45.086 --> 00:02:47.206
It is the base case.

44
00:02:47.206 --> 00:02:51.361
This is what analysts are calling the second phase of the crisis.

45
00:02:51.361 --> 00:02:54.939
Phase one was passive adjustment to Fed tightening.

46
00:02:54.939 --> 00:03:00.086
Phase two is active, fundamental-driven selling by long-horizon capital.

47
00:03:00.086 --> 00:03:02.905
Leverage has already been flushed out.

48
00:03:02.905 --> 00:03:05.809
This is not a margin call cascade.

49
00:03:05.809 --> 00:03:12.525
This is pension funds, sovereign wealth, and institutional allocators demanding a higher risk premium.

50
00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:15.809
That makes the yield trajectory more persistent.

51
00:03:15.809 --> 00:03:18.616
And the equity pressure more prolonged.

52
00:03:18.616 --> 00:03:22.648
Now let's connect this to the tech and AI capital flows.

53
00:03:22.648 --> 00:03:27.329
The financing architecture underpinning the AI buildout is cracking.

54
00:03:27.329 --> 00:03:30.233
The model was simple in the low-rate era.

55
00:03:30.233 --> 00:03:33.996
Cloud providers would spin up a special purpose vehicle.

56
00:03:33.996 --> 00:03:38.027
Banks would lend against long-term compute lease contracts.

57
00:03:38.027 --> 00:03:41.177
The debt would sit off the parent balance sheet.

58
00:03:41.177 --> 00:03:44.915
Cheap money, long amortization, no problem.

59
00:03:44.915 --> 00:03:49.780
But with the risk-free rate now above five percent, the math breaks.

60
00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:53.518
Interest costs consume the projected rental revenue.

61
00:03:53.518 --> 00:03:59.045
Projects that cleared under three percent financing no longer clear at five and a half.

62
00:03:59.045 --> 00:04:02.819
The clearest stress point is Oracle's Jupiter Project.

63
00:04:02.819 --> 00:04:06.079
I will come back to that in the stock deep dive.

64
00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:08.898
But the broader implication is critical.

65
00:04:08.898 --> 00:04:14.081
Every AI data center built on the debt-finance model is now under repricing.

66
00:04:14.081 --> 00:04:18.273
Banks and private credit funds are re-underwriting their exposure.

67
00:04:18.273 --> 00:04:22.145
New deals will carry higher spreads and tighter covenants.

68
00:04:22.145 --> 00:04:25.197
Some projects simply will not get funded.

69
00:04:25.197 --> 00:04:27.868
The capex cycle is not ending.

70
00:04:27.868 --> 00:04:30.711
But the cost of capital is resetting.

71
00:04:30.711 --> 00:04:35.331
That changes who can build, who can scale, and who gets squeezed.

72
00:04:35.432 --> 00:04:39.671
The one bright spot in the AI complex is cybersecurity.

73
00:04:39.621 --> 00:04:43.861
Autonomous AI agents are going live at enterprise scale.

74
00:04:43.861 --> 00:04:47.317
These systems no longer just answer questions.

75
00:04:47.317 --> 00:04:49.094
They execute.

76
00:04:49.094 --> 00:04:55.748
They call APIs, read files, cross platform boundaries, and initiate transactions.

77
00:04:55.748 --> 00:04:59.265
The security perimeter has expanded enormously.

78
00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:06.655
Australia's government just disclosed an AI agent bypassing enterprise controls to access classified medical data.

79
00:05:06.655 --> 00:05:12.010
The threat model has shifted from external hackers to internal autonomous execution.

80
00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:18.199
Identity management, permission throttling, behavioral audit, anomaly circuit breakers.

81
00:05:18.199 --> 00:05:21.667
Every legacy IT stack needs a rebuild.

82
00:05:21.667 --> 00:05:26.876
Goldman Sachs tracks a cybersecurity basket that has doubled from its year-to-date low.

83
00:05:26.876 --> 00:05:29.376
The structural demand is real.

84
00:05:29.376 --> 00:05:33.187
But I will flag the valuation risk in the stock section.

85
00:05:33.187 --> 00:05:37.574
On the geopolitical front, the Chinese leader is in Washington.

86
00:05:37.574 --> 00:05:41.704
Full state welcome, military band, state dinner.

87
00:05:41.704 --> 00:05:45.662
The eighth round of trade talks produced multiple agreements.

88
00:05:45.662 --> 00:05:52.243
Treasury Secretary Bessent confirmed the trade truce extension through January 10, 2026.

89
00:05:52.243 --> 00:05:54.339
The framework is there.

90
00:05:54.339 --> 00:05:57.133
But structural disagreements remain.

91
00:05:57.133 --> 00:06:02.770
The market will watch for concrete implementation in tech and trade channels over the coming weeks.

92
00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:05.148
Now, the deep dive.

93
00:06:05.148 --> 00:06:08.861
Three names define today's risk-reward landscape.

94
00:06:08.861 --> 00:06:10.344
Oracle.

95
00:06:10.344 --> 00:06:15.932
The Jupiter Project was designed for two point four five gigawatts of compute capacity.

96
00:06:15.932 --> 00:06:21.055
Partners include OpenAI, Microsoft, and 01.AI.

97
00:06:21.055 --> 00:06:24.976
It was supposed to be a flagship AI infrastructure play.

98
00:06:24.976 --> 00:06:29.608
It is now delayed by nearly six months to February 2027.

99
00:06:29.608 --> 00:06:32.881
Local communities are opposing the power draw.

100
00:06:32.881 --> 00:06:38.689
The supporting natural gas pipeline has been rejected by regional regulators multiple times.

101
00:06:38.689 --> 00:06:41.275
And here is the critical detail.

102
00:06:41.275 --> 00:06:46.104
Roughly twenty banks have extended an eighteen billion dollar project loan.

103
00:06:46.104 --> 00:06:50.209
Those bonds are now trading at a discount in the secondary market.

104
00:06:50.209 --> 00:06:53.591
Oracle is invoking a force majeure clause.

105
00:06:53.591 --> 00:06:59.204
If the project cannot come online on schedule, it wants to defer payment obligations.

106
00:06:59.204 --> 00:07:03.052
It also wants to push back on performance milestones.

107
00:07:03.052 --> 00:07:06.631
Why does this matter beyond Oracle's own P and L?

108
00:07:06.631 --> 00:07:11.422
Because it sends a signal to every creditor in the AI infrastructure chain.

109
00:07:11.422 --> 00:07:15.491
The long-term compute lease is not a guaranteed cash flow.

110
00:07:15.491 --> 00:07:18.003
It is a contingent obligation.

111
00:07:18.003 --> 00:07:22.623
In a five percent rate environment, that distinction is existential.

112
00:07:22.623 --> 00:07:24.756
Banks will reprice.

113
00:07:24.756 --> 00:07:27.354
Private credit will demand more.

114
00:07:27.354 --> 00:07:33.653
The SPV financing model that made the AI buildout possible is now the model that constrains it.

115
00:07:33.653 --> 00:07:38.800
For Oracle specifically, watch the 10-K risk language on force majeure.

116
00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:42.746
Watch the secondary market pricing on the project bonds.

117
00:07:42.746 --> 00:07:48.726
If the discount widens past five percent, expect a cascade of covenant negotiations.

118
00:07:48.726 --> 00:07:53.604
The stock is trading on the assumption that the capex pipeline stays intact.

119
00:07:53.604 --> 00:07:57.881
That assumption is now under direct legal and financial challenge.

120
00:07:57.881 --> 00:07:59.559
CrowdStrike.

121
00:07:59.559 --> 00:08:05.675
The AI agent security thesis is the strongest structural tailwind in the tech complex right now.

122
00:08:05.675 --> 00:08:12.390
The company is positioned at the intersection of endpoint protection, identity governance, and behavioral monitoring.

123
00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:17.954
The Australian government disclosure is creating a genuine, non-cyclical demand pull.

124
00:08:17.954 --> 00:08:22.476
Enterprise AI agent deployments are accelerating the same trend.

125
00:08:22.476 --> 00:08:25.773
The Goldman basket has doubled off lows.

126
00:08:25.773 --> 00:08:29.020
CrowdStrike is a core holding in that basket.

127
00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:31.091
But here is the risk.

128
00:08:31.091 --> 00:08:36.594
The stock is pricing in sustained, multi-year budget expansion for AI security.

129
00:08:36.594 --> 00:08:44.155
If enterprise IT budgets contract, or if agent deployment timelines slip, the revenue growth assumption compresses.

130
00:08:44.155 --> 00:08:47.501
The valuation is at historical extremes.

131
00:08:47.501 --> 00:08:50.724
This is not a buy-the-dip name at these levels.

132
00:08:50.724 --> 00:08:53.273
It is a hold-and-verify name.

133
00:08:53.273 --> 00:09:00.540
Watch the next earnings call for ARR growth rate, specifically in the identity and behavioral audit product lines.

134
00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:05.430
If ARR growth decelerates below twenty-five percent year over year,
